hi everybody i'm william and today i'm  talking to you about pseudorandomness  right so a pseudorandom generator  is a function that stretches a short  truly random seed out to a long  pseudorandom string and and the way we  make this pseudorandomness condition  precise  is that we we consider some class of  functions this the script f  and we require that  that this that the output of this  generator looks random to every function  in that class okay so the acceptance  probability of a function in that class  should be approximately the same whether  we're plugging in truly random bits or  pseudorandom bits  and maybe the most traditional setting  would be if we take this this class  script app to be like everything that  can be computed in polynomial time or  something like that  but for today actually we're more  interested in the case that this class  script f is like very restricted  and  the benefit of looking at a very  restricted class of functions is that  then we can actually prove something we  can design pseudorandom generators that  provably work so like maybe the simplest  possible example uh here's a  pseudorandom generator that stretches  two bits out to three bits  right but the class of functions that  we're going to fool are just uh depth  two decision trees on these three bits  so you know a little toy model and the  generator says you take these two  the two bits of the seed x and y and  then you output x and then y and then  the xor of x and y right this is like  the classic example of a distribution  that's pairwise independent which means  in particular that it will fool these  depth two decision trees things that  only look at two bits  so that's just like a very simple you  know toy examples you know trying to  illustrate uh this concept and that it  doesn't necessarily have to be you know  like a conditional thing where we make  complexity theoretic assumptions or  anything like that we can make you know  concrete constructions of pseudorandom  generators that that work for for simple  sort of combinatorial reasons  potentially  um  but of course you know we're really more  interested in in uh classes f that are  maybe a little more rich than something  you know  overly simplistic like this and so  for example um just to give you some  examples of my research on this topic  with collaborators  um so we've we've designed like explicit  pseudorandom generators for uh  restricted models of computation like  read once bounded depth circuits  or  bounded depth threshold circuits  or certain kinds of  branching programs things like this and  so when you restrict the model then you  know hopefully we get these models of  computation there that are hopefully  still interesting they're rich enough to  do something something interesting uh  but they're weak enough that we can  actually fool them  so let me try to give you some some  motivation for for why would we why  would be interested in uh theorems like  the ones that i had on the previous  slide you know why design pseudorandom  generators for these models  well the most basic reason to design a  pseudorandom generator is to try to  decrease the number of random bits that  you're using for some computational  process  okay right because if you're using a  pseudorandom generator  now the only random bits that we need  are the seed of that generator  okay and so we want to think of random  bits as being like a scarce  computational resource just like time or  space so just like a faster algorithm is  better than a slower algorithm  we want to think of an algorithm that  uses fewer random bits as being better  than an algorithm that uses more random  bits  and best of all is an algorithm that  doesn't use any randomness right a  deterministic algorithm and that's also  sometimes i mean depending on the  context that might be something that you  can achieve with a pseudorandom  generator because  to try to you know to get rid of this  last little bit of randomness the seed  of the generator uh we can potent i mean  depending on the context we can  potentially  just try all seeds and see what happens  you know for every single one of those  seeds  another reason to design pseudorandom  generators is that actually uh sometimes  they're useful uh for the for designing  randomized algorithms  um just you know in some contexts uh uh  this comes up like streaming algorithms  is maybe a classic  example so that's so that seems a little  bit like the opposite of the previous  goals but yeah  and and and maybe actually the most  important reason is a little bit subtler  uh which is just that when we design a  pseudorandom generator for some class of  functions f if we're able to design an  unconditional generator you know a  construction that we can truly just  write down and analyze  then that really sheds a lot of light on  this class f it teaches us something  about this model of computation  so you can think of a pseudo-random  generator as like a highly refined type  of lower bound for this model it's  giving like an example of a task that  this model of computation cannot solve  at all it doesn't understand it at all  right because the pseudorandom generator  is sampling some distribution that's  actually you know a very low entropy  distribution but it looks totally random  to this class f so it's like the class f  has you know no understanding of the  structure that is actually  present um so just to give you some idea  of like what sort of problems there are  to work on in this area  um really one of the biggest uh open  problems is to try to design uh better  pseudorandom generators for space  bounded computation this this problem uh  drives and motivates like a lot of the  research in this area  um  maybe some potentially easier problems  hopefully easier problems would be like  even even for simple models of  computation like read once cns okay so  this is an and of ors of variables and  each variable only happens one time okay  or literals i should say you know we  allow not gates also and even for that  like very simple model of computation we  still don't have optimal pseudorandom  generators we have very close to optimal  but we're not quite there yet um  and then there are also you know  interesting you know other you know  somewhere in the middle there are  interesting models of like founded depth  uh read once circuits of various types  where we can hope to design very good  pseudorandom generators and right now we  only have pretty good superheated  generators  okay so so let me slightly move on to it  you know very related topic which is  deer randomization it already came up  one time right one of the  reasons to design pseudorandom  generators is to try to de-randomize  algorithms which i think is is a  also just you know a nice goal in its  own right  um  so as a reminder  this class bp space s  is the class of languages that can be  decided by a randomized algorithm that  uses of s bits of space  and the conjecture the popular  conjecture says that bp space s  coincides with deterministic space s  right so this conjecture is saying that  randomness is not actually necessary for  space efficient computation so you know  you might as well take all your fair  coins and throw them in the trash can  um and so if you've ever studied you  know complexity theory at all uh you  know that there are a lot of conjectures  like this you know we have all sorts of  ideas but what we think is true about uh  the relationships between you know  different fundamental uh computational  resources like in this case space and  randomness um  and in most cases these problems are you  know really extremely difficult and and  you know people have been working on  them for a long time with very little  success one thing that sets this problem  apart a little bit is that i i would  argue that there's more hope of actually  proving this compared to you know some  of the other similar uh you know  superficially similar similar  conjectures in complexity theory  um and one piece of evidence for that  you know one reason to maybe have some  hope about this problem is that we do  have uh quite a few partial results so  i'll just mention a couple partial  results that you know that are my own  research but but there are plenty of  other examples that i have nothing to do  with  um  so one would be uh you know we can at  least say all right if we have vp space  s we can de-randomize that with all  right there's some blow up in space but  we can bound the you know the extent to  which the space complexity blows up  and and yes apparently the best bound is  is s to the three halves over square  root log s which is just this you know  this this tiny improvement over uh you  know an older result from the 90s  they've got this best of the three  halves found  or just you know one one more example  would be uh if we if we restrict the  class a little more so we we're not  looking at uh just bounding space but  let's bounce time and space so let's  look at um  problems that can be decided by  randomized algorithms that  simultaneously use quasi-linear time and  logarithmic space  okay then it turns out it's possible to  de-randomize those in log space the  deterministic algorithm is no longer  fast but  uh and then there's this extra caveat  which is that it's only like a typically  correct algorithm  it gives the wrong answer sometimes but  okay so just to wrap up um  i just want to say i mean i don't think  we have really any excuse for having not  yet you know proven uh unconditionally  that vp space s equals deterministic  space s so i'd love to work on this  problem with with any of you  um and let me mention just quickly you  know a couple of problems that are  potentially easier on the way that i  think are fascinating would be like  proving that bpl is contained in nl or  maybe proving that rl is closed under  complement  but anyway thanks for listening and 